#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063826 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "da0a95e3-c020-59c0-a3ca-4d809fea1224" :question/math-academy-id "e-10002" :question/problem "Which of the curves above are concave up?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/363/Source/Images/8492ba35b1e03c9d202786434dd3bec0.png)" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOnly graph II curves upward (like a \"smile\") among the given graphs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063827 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "1869f910-361f-58c4-8076-5c4386d702f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-10003" :question/problem "The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* $f'(x) = 0$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/363/Source/Images/a372677270083a97ac62a65afed96f4f.png)" :question/worked-solution "The derivative $f'(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/363/Source/Images/7d64202ec8938c64fa2c7a4396c6e3ee.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d$, the graph is concave up ($\\smile$) at $x=b$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064037 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6a860b08-3913-54d7-a3d6-ec15ce4553b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6806" :question/problem "The graph above shows the function $y=f(x)$, defined on $[-3,3]$. Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/363/Source/Images/d8ca4ba12984a3af9c8972837d567848.png)" :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\\smile$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\\frown$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downward like a frown ($\\color{black}{\\frown}$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/363/Source/Images/0d75d271b710814d595b25ae7d980605.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd5c660-b216-59b4-b2bb-df83ef864b2a" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concave Up and Concave Down Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fe04ab4-25c0-5c68-b1b6-924958447957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061193} {:answer/id #uuid "296af852-54a2-51f7-8f2a-754218d7dfa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id